---
layout: layouts/base.njk
title: Chart lab
description: The site's chart grammar, rendered on real cartebase data, with variants to compare.
permalink: /lab/charts/
hidden: true
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
collectionArchetype: ledger
---

{% set listening = cartebase.listening %}
{% set photoAes = cartebase.photo_aesthetics %}
{% set pageEyebrow = "Hidden workbench" %}
{% set pageTitle = "Chart lab" %}
{% set pageDescription = "Every chart on the site is SVG rendered at build time by one shortcode, <code>&#123;% chart %&#125;</code>. Two choices are still open: the ink wobble on annotation rings and leaders, and the halftone dot fills. The default turns both on. A variant follows you around the site for this tab until you turn it off." %}
{% set itemCount = 8 %}
{% set itemLabel = "chart types" %}
{% include "components/page-header.njk" %}

<table class="font-lab-table">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Variant</th><th>What changes</th><th>Try it on</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {% set samples = [{ label: "this page", url: "/lab/charts/" }, { label: "/data/", url: "/data/" }, { label: "/listening/", url: "/listening/" }] %}
    {% for v in [
      { id: "off", label: "Ink and dots (default)", note: "Hand-drawn wobble on annotation rings and leaders. Areas and heatmaps are halftone dots, sized by value." },
      { id: "dots", label: "Dots only", note: "Halftone fills, but every line is ruled straight." },
      { id: "ink", label: "Ink only", note: "Wobble on, areas as a flat accent tint, heatmap as stepped squares." },
      { id: "plain", label: "Plain", note: "Precise lines, flat tint, stepped squares. The quietest version." }
    ] %}
    <tr>
      <td><b>{{ v.label }}</b><br><code>?chart={{ v.id }}</code></td>
      <td>{{ v.note }}</td>
      <td>{% for s in samples %}<a href="{{ s.url }}?chart={{ v.id }}">{{ s.label }}</a>{% if not loop.last %} · {% endif %}{% endfor %}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 class="editorial-title">Line, with annotations</h2>
<p class="editorial-support">For change over time. The title states the finding. One or two notes in Fraunces italic point at the data. Hover a column for its value. Every chart has a footer with source, n and date range, plus the data table and query behind it.</p>

{% chart {
  type: "line",
  data: listening.plays_per_month,
  x: "month",
  y: "plays",
  area: true,
  unit: "plays",
  kicker: "Spotify · plays per month",
  title: "2024 was the loudest year, peaking in November",
  notes: [{ at: "2024-11", text: "4,279 plays,\nthe busiest month", dx: -170, dy: -4 }],
  source: "cartebase · spotify_listening_history",
  n: listening.plays_total,
  range: "Oct 2016 – Apr 2026",
  xLabel: "month"
} %}

{% chart {
  type: "line",
  data: photoAes.monthly,
  x: "month",
  y: "mean",
  yMin: 0.3,
  yMax: 0.55,
  digits: 3,
  count: "photos",
  countLabel: "photos",
  highlight: ["2026-01", "2026-04"],
  kicker: "Apple Photos · mean aesthetic score, by month",
  title: "Context in grey, the point in accent: 2026 so far is only winter",
  notes: [{ at: "2026-02", text: "Jan–Apr only", dx: -40, dy: 40 }],
  ref: { value: photoAes.mean, label: "all-time mean " ~ photoAes.mean },
  source: "cartebase · photos_assets",
  n: photoAes.photos,
  range: "2022 – Apr 2026",
  query: photoAes.sql.monthly
} %}

<h2 class="editorial-title">Bars</h2>
<p class="editorial-support">For magnitude across categories or bins. Use <code>highlight</code> to pick out the bar the sentence is about; the rest go grey and get no labels.</p>

{% chart {
  type: "bars",
  data: photoAes.histogram,
  x: "bucket",
  y: "count",
  highlight: "max",
  xTicks: 10,
  kicker: "Apple Photos · score distribution",
  title: "The typical photo scores about " ~ photoAes.median,
  notes: [{ at: "0.65", text: "the top 1% start\nat " ~ photoAes.p99, dx: 60, dy: -50 }],
  source: "cartebase · photos_assets",
  n: photoAes.photos,
  xLabel: "score bucket",
  yLabel: "photos"
} %}

<h2 class="editorial-title">Ranked rows</h2>
<p class="editorial-support">Replaces horizontal bar charts. Built in HTML so long names wrap instead of getting cut off. The first row is in accent unless <code>highlight</code> says otherwise.</p>

{% chart {
  type: "rank",
  data: listening.top_artists_all_time,
  x: "artist",
  y: "plays",
  total: listening.plays_total,
  limit: 8,
  kicker: "Spotify · top artists, all time",
  title: "Radiohead, by some distance",
  source: "cartebase · spotify_listening_history",
  n: listening.plays_total
} %}

<h2 class="editorial-title">Heatmap</h2>
<p class="editorial-support">Halftone by default: the area of each dot scales with the value. Plain and ink-only variants switch to squares stepped by opacity.</p>

{% chart {
  type: "heatmap",
  matrix: listening.hour_weekday_heatmap,
  unit: "plays",
  kicker: "Spotify · hour × weekday (UTC)",
  title: "Saturday leads; the busy hours fall late in the UTC day",
  source: "cartebase · spotify_listening_history",
  n: listening.plays_total
} %}

<h2 class="editorial-title">Waffle</h2>
<p class="editorial-support">One dot per percent. For a single share that needs to feel like a share.</p>

{% chart {
  type: "waffle",
  value: listening.top_artists_all_time[0].share_pct,
  label: "of every track I've streamed since 2016 is " ~ listening.top_artists_all_time[0].artist ~ ".",
  kicker: "Spotify · share of plays",
  source: "cartebase · spotify_listening_history",
  n: listening.plays_total,
  table: false
} %}

<h2 class="editorial-title">Small multiples</h2>
<p class="editorial-support">Replaces stacked areas with a rainbow of hues. Each series gets its own panel on a shared scale, so one accent is enough.</p>

{% chart {
  type: "multiples",
  labels: listening.artist_share_per_year.years,
  series: listening.artist_share_per_year.series,
  unit: "%",
  digits: 1,
  limit: 9,
  start: "2019",
  kicker: "Spotify · share of each year's plays",
  title: "Radiohead arrived in 2024 and has grown every year since",
  caption: "From 2019; 2016–2018 hold under 50 plays in total.",
  source: "cartebase · spotify_listening_history",
  n: listening.plays_total
} %}

<h2 class="editorial-title">Steps</h2>
<p class="editorial-support">The old <code>{% raw %}{% progression_chart %}{% endraw %}</code>, now drawn by the same renderer. For a few measured values in sequence, each one labelled.</p>

{% progression_chart {
  kicker: "Example · tokens per second",
  title: "Each change, measured",
  unit: "t/s",
  points: [
    { val: 6.5, lbl: "Baseline", sub: "stock" },
    { val: 11.2, lbl: "Flash attn", sub: "+FA" },
    { val: 14.9, lbl: "Offload", sub: "experts on CPU" },
    { val: 20.8, lbl: "Tuned", sub: "MTP draft", highlight: true }
  ],
  caption: "Illustrative values for the specimen, not a benchmark."
} %}

<h2 class="editorial-title">Sparkline</h2>
<p class="editorial-support">Inline, inside a stat tile. It has no frame or footer, because the tile already gives the context.</p>

<div class="not-prose grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-3 mb-10">
  {% from "components/cartebase-charts.njk" import stat_tile %}
  {{ stat_tile("Spotify plays", listening.plays_total | localeNumber, "since 2016", chartSvg({ type: "spark", data: listening.plays_per_month, x: "month", y: "plays" })) }}
  {{ stat_tile("Photos scored", photoAes.photos | localeNumber, "2022 onward", chartSvg({ type: "spark", data: photoAes.monthly, x: "month", y: "photos" })) }}
</div>

<p class="editorial-support">Options and examples are documented in <code>docs/BLOG_POST_FEATURES.md</code> under Charts. The renderer is <code>utils/charts.mjs</code>.</p>
